<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- GP-6 — the negative-control proof: deliberately non-conforming fake IDeviceDriver implementations
         (see Fakes/) plus the tests proving DeviceDriverConformanceSuite's checks actually REJECT each one.
         Plain net10.0, matching St4i.Connector.Conformance.csproj itself — no St4i.EdgeCore/EngineApi in
         reach, same reasoning as St4i.Connector.Abstractions.Tests. -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Test-hygiene batch — the shared, disposable scratch root every test assembly installs before its
         first test runs (see tests/Shared/TestRunTempRoot.cs for the full rationale and the measured
         numbers). LINKED, not copied: one module initializer per assembly, one definition to maintain.
         Without it this suite writes into the real %TEMP% and the real %ProgramData%\ST4I\sim\creds and
         never cleans up either. -->
    <Compile Include="..\Shared\TestRunTempRoot.cs" Link="TestRunTempRoot.cs" />
    <!-- Task K-1 — the guard that measures the CONSEQUENCE rather than the naming: the REAL
         %ProgramData%\ST4I\sim\creds must not GAIN a file while this assembly's process runs (see
         tests/Shared/RealCredentialStoreLeakGuard.cs for what it does and does not see). LINKED into all
         five suites for the same reason as the line above — each process is its own measurement, and a
         guard installed in one suite says nothing about the other four.

         TWO THINGS ABOUT THIS PROJECT'S DELIBERATE ONE-REFERENCE BOUNDARY, and the second is a real cost
         rather than a footnote — the first draft of this comment asserted the first and introduced the
         second in the same clause (re-review Minor):
           * ASSEMBLY boundary: UNTOUCHED. The guard's only `using`s are System.* and Xunit; it adds no
             ProjectReference and cannot see St4i.EdgeCore.
           * TWO NEW DEPENDENCIES, and the first draft of this bullet named only one (branch review,
             Important 3):
               (1) THE SOURCE TREE — it derives the directory by READING the product source, so off-tree
                   execution yields SetupFailure and a RED suite.
               (2) READ ACCESS TO A HARDENED MACHINE-WIDE DIRECTORY — it enumerates
                   %ProgramData%\ST4I\sim\creds, which SecurityDirAcl.Apply restricts to
                   SYSTEM/Administrators/owner on every CredentialStore.Save.
             Both fail closed and both are deliberate; the alternatives are a path literal that goes
             silently vacuous when the store moves, and treating "I could not look" as "nothing was
             there". Deliberate is not free, which is why both are written down. -->
    <Compile Include="..\Shared\RealCredentialStoreLeakGuard.cs" Link="RealCredentialStoreLeakGuard.cs" />
    <!-- Task X-1 — the same consequence-measuring shape turned on the OTHER directory that survives a run:
         this assembly's OWN OUTPUT DIRECTORY, which `dotnet build` does not clean and which three of the
         product's stores resolve to by default. LINKED into all five — the property is "no test process
         changes its own output directory", and this project has nothing there today only because nothing
         has yet asked it to.

         AGAINST THIS PROJECT'S ONE-REFERENCE BOUNDARY, measured the same way as the line above:
           * ASSEMBLY boundary: UNTOUCHED. The guard's only `using`s are System.* and Xunit; it adds no
             ProjectReference and cannot see St4i.EdgeCore or St4i.EngineApi — which is exactly why it
             names the two exempt stores by SOURCE PATH rather than by `cref`.
           * NO NEW DEPENDENCY BEYOND (1) ABOVE. It reads the product source for the same reason and with
             the same fail-closed behaviour, so this project's off-tree requirement is unchanged in kind.
             It needs no access to any machine-wide directory: it watches only its own bin output. -->
    <Compile Include="..\Shared\OwnOutputDirectoryGuard.cs" Link="OwnOutputDirectoryGuard.cs" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.11.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit" Version="2.9.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="2.8.2" />
  </ItemGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\src\St4i.Connector.Conformance\St4i.Connector.Conformance.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
